@*
    One collapsible group in the filter panel.

    Collapsible because a real library has more camera bodies and folders than fit on screen at
    once, and a panel that needs scrolling past six sections to reach the seventh is worse than
    no panel.

    Sections start CLOSED. Open, this panel put roughly eighty rows of "label ......... number"
    down the left-hand third of the window, and a right-aligned column of eighty numbers reads as
    a ledger — it demands to be read, and it was competing with the photographs for attention in
    an application whose entire job is showing photographs. Closed, the same panel is seven
    section names: the structure is still visible, nothing is hidden, and one click brings any of
    it back.

    A section holding an active filter opens itself, because a filter you cannot see is a filter
    you cannot turn off.
*@

<section class="fsection @(_open ? "open" : null)">
    <button type="button"
            class="fsection-head"
            aria-expanded="@(_open ? "true" : "false")"
            @onclick="Toggle">
        <span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">@(_open ? "▾" : "▸")</span>
        <span class="t">@Title</span>
        @if (Count > 0)
        {
            <span class="n">@Count</span>
        }
    </button>

    @if (_open)
    {
        <div class="fsection-body">
            @ChildContent
        </div>
    }
</section>

@code {
    /// <summary>Section heading.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>How many distinct values this section offers. Zero hides the badge.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public int Count { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The rows in the section.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether this section holds a filter that is currently applied.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Such a section opens itself and stays open. The alternative — a narrowed library with the
    /// reason folded away behind a closed door — is the worst state this panel can be in.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Active { get; set; }

    private bool _open;
    private bool _touched;

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        // The user's own choice wins once they have made one; until then, being active is what
        // decides. Without the guard, clearing a filter would snap the section shut under the
        // pointer that just cleared it.
        if (!_touched)
        {
            _open = Active;
        }
    }

    private void Toggle()
    {
        _touched = true;
        _open = !_open;
    }
}
